--TEST--
`Core\Bytes::unpack`, `::at` and `::fill` each name the last octet they read beside the first they cannot — spec § 7, `rule:types/bytes`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4 and R8
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 7's three reading boundaries, each asserted on both sides and by the
// *message* it names rather than by the fact that something threw: a member
// that stops one octet early prints plausibly against either half alone.
//
// `bytes-unpack-reads-back-what-pack-wrote.nvst` pins the round trip and
// `bytes-indexes-and-orders-by-byte-offset.nvst` the in-range answers; this
// case pins where each stops. `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/bytes.rs` owns the four
// sites — `advanced`, the leftover check at the foot of `unpacked`,
// `nvs_core_bytes_at` and `nvs_core_bytes_fill`.

final class Buf {
    public static function why(bytes $b, string $format): string {
        try {
            Core\Bytes::unpack($b, $format);
            return "";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function ok(bytes $b, string $format): string {
        if (Buf::why($b, $format) == "") {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }

    public static function fields(bytes $b, string $format): tainted string {
        try {
            return Core\Json::encode(Core\Bytes::unpack($b, $format));
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "refused";
        }
    }

    public static function at(bytes $b, int $index): string {
        try {
            return Core\Bytes::at($b, $index) as string;
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function fill(uint $length, uint $byte): string {
        try {
            return Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::fill($length, $byte));
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }
}

var $quad = Core\Encoding::fromHex("00112233");

// A code reads its whole width or none of it, and the refusal names the code,
// that width, the offset it was reading at and what the buffer actually holds
// — the four things a caller needs to see which field of a record ran out.
echo Buf::fields($quad, "N"), "\n";
echo Buf::why(Core\Bytes::slice($quad, 0, 3), "N"), "\n";

// Counted over every width in the table: a buffer of exactly the code's width
// is one field, and one octet short is refused. A code that read what was there
// and zero-extended the rest keeps the first count and loses the second.
array<string> $codes = ["C", "n", "N", "J", "G", "E"];
array<int> $widths = [1, 2, 4, 8, 4, 8];
int $fits = 0;
int $short = 0;
foreach ($codes as string $k => string $c) {
    var $w = $widths[$k];
    if (Buf::ok(Core\Bytes::fill($w as uint, 0), $c) == "y") {
        $fits = $fits + 1;
    }
    if (Buf::ok(Core\Bytes::fill(($w - 1) as uint, 0), $c) == "n") {
        $short = $short + 1;
    }
}
echo $fits, " ", $short, " of ", Core\Arr::count($codes), "\n";

// The same bound read along the buffer rather than across one field: `x` skips
// octets, so `x3C` reads at the last offset a one-octet code has and `x4C` at
// the first it does not. The message counts the offset, not the field.
echo Buf::fields($quad, "x3C"), " ", Buf::why($quad, "x4C"), "\n";

// Octets the format never reads are a throw and not a silence, because a format
// is a description of the buffer (the member's own doc comment, against PHP's
// `unpack`, which ignores them). The message names the remedy, and the remedy
// works: slicing the header off first is one of the three ways to describe the
// whole of a five-octet packet, and all three read the same header.
var $packet = Core\Encoding::fromHex("0000002a99");
echo Buf::why($packet, "N"), "\n";
echo Buf::fields(Core\Bytes::slice($packet, 0, 4), "N"), " ", Buf::fields($packet, "Nx"), " ", Buf::fields($packet, "NC"), "\n";
echo Buf::why(Core\Encoding::fromHex("0102"), ""), "\n";

// `Core\Bytes::at` asks the strict question `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R8's range rule leaves the
// window members answering leniently: the last index each way is an octet and
// the first past it is a throw, on both signs, and an empty buffer has no index
// at all rather than a sentinel one.
echo Buf::at($quad, 3), " ", Buf::at($quad, -4), "\n";
echo Buf::at($quad, 4), "\n";
echo Buf::at($quad, -5), "\n";
echo Buf::at(Core\Encoding::fromHex(""), 0), "\n";

// The far ends of the index type itself, which is where the counting-from-the-
// end arithmetic could wrap back into range rather than refuse: `i64::MAX`
// addresses nothing, and `i64::MIN` cannot be added to the length at all. The
// low one is computed rather than written, because a literal past `int` is a
// separate refusal and would hide this one.
int $most = 9223372036854775807;
int $least = 0 - $most - 1;
echo Buf::at($quad, $most), "\n";
echo Buf::at($quad, $least), "\n";

// Counted: what makes those two ends the same bound is that the two spellings
// name one octet, so all four indices agree with their negatives.
int $same = 0;
for (int $i = 0; $i < 4; $i = $i + 1) {
    if (Core\Bytes::at($quad, $i) == Core\Bytes::at($quad, $i - 4)) {
        $same = $same + 1;
    }
}
echo $same, " of 4\n";

// `Core\Bytes::fill`'s byte is a `uint`, so only its top end is a runtime
// bound: 255 is the last octet and 256 is a caller's bug rather than `chr()`'s
// wrap to `"\0"` (`rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4). The low end never becomes an argument — a
// negative literal is `E0401` at the call, exactly as `pack`'s 64-bit codes
// leave their range to the compiler.
echo Buf::fill(2, 255), "\n";
echo Buf::fill(2, 256), "\n";

// Counted: every value the bound admits is one octet `Core\Bytes::at` reads
// back, which is what makes 255 the last one rather than an arbitrary cut.
int $held = 0;
for (int $v = 0; $v < 256; $v = $v + 1) {
    if (Core\Bytes::at(Core\Bytes::fill(1, $v as uint), 0) == $v as uint) {
        $held = $held + 1;
    }
}
echo $held, " of 256\n";
--EXPECT--
[1122867]
Core\Bytes::unpack: `N` reads 4 octets at offset 0, and the buffer holds 3
6 6 of 6
[51] Core\Bytes::unpack: `C` reads 1 octets at offset 4, and the buffer holds 4
Core\Bytes::unpack: the format reads 4 of the buffer's 5 octets — a header read off a longer buffer is `Core\Bytes::slice` and then this
[42] [42] [42,153]
Core\Bytes::unpack: the format reads 0 of the buffer's 2 octets — a header read off a longer buffer is `Core\Bytes::slice` and then this
51 0
Core\Bytes::at: index 4 is outside a buffer of 4 bytes
Core\Bytes::at: index -5 is outside a buffer of 4 bytes
Core\Bytes::at: index 0 is outside a buffer of 0 bytes
Core\Bytes::at: index 9223372036854775807 is outside a buffer of 4 bytes
Core\Bytes::at: index -9223372036854775808 is outside a buffer of 4 bytes
4 of 4
ffff
Core\Bytes::fill: 256 is not one octet — a byte is 0 to 255
256 of 256
